sruption leads to impaired ion homeostasis and increased neuronal excitability, which are believed to trigger the neurological symptoms of hemiplegic migraine. The clinical features of FHM2 are indistinguishable from those of other hemiplegic migraine subtypes, including motor weakness, visual aura, and headache. The presence of ATP1A2 mutations supports a genetic diagnosis and aids in family screening and risk assessment. [Data: Entities (462); Relationships (606)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"ATP1A2 gene mutations are a known cause of familial hemiplegic migraine type 2\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"SCN1A is a gene linked to familial hemiplegic migraine type 3 (FHM3), a third genetically defined form of the condition. Mutations in SCN1A affect the function of voltage-gated sodium channels in neurons, which are essential for the initiation and propagation of action potentials. These mutations can lead to neuronal hyperexcitability and abnormal electrical activity in the brain, contributing to the onset of migraine attacks with motor aura. The clinical presentation of FHM3 is consistent with other forms of hemiplegic migraine, including temporary paralysis, sensory disturbances, and cognitive symptoms. The identification of SCN1A mutations provides a genetic basis for diagnosis and supports targeted genetic testing in families with a history of the condition. [Data: Entities (463); Relationships (607)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"SCN1A gene mutations are a known cause of familial hemiplegic migraine type 3\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Sporadic hemiplegic migraine (SHM) is a form of hemiplegic migraine that occurs in individuals without a family history of the condition. It is clinically defined by the absence of first- or second-degree relatives who also experience hemiplegic migraine attacks with motor weakness. Despite the lack of a familial pattern, the clinical symptoms of SHM are identical to those of 